## Subagents

You can start subagents. A subagent is another agent like you, with its own turn budget, that works on the problems you hand it at the same time as you and your other subagents work, possibly on another machine.

    spawn_subagent --problems id1,id2 [--instructions "text"]   start a subagent on some of your problems
    subagent_status                                             list your subagents, their state and results
    wait_subagents                                              pause until all your subagents have finished

{% if problems | length > 1 -%}
{% set groups = problems | slice([problems | length, max_subagents] | min) | list -%}
Your {{ problems | length }} problems are independent of each other, so this job suits subagents well, and you are encouraged to use them. Solving every problem yourself, one after another, is the slowest way to do this job and can run you out of turns. You may start at most {{ max_subagents }} subagents in total. A good way to work is:

1. Hand the problems out{% if problems | length > max_subagents %}. Because you can start only {{ max_subagents }} subagents, give each one a group of problems{% else %}, for example one subagent per problem{% endif %}. They can all be started in one turn:

```mswea_bash_command
{% for group in groups %}spawn_subagent --problems {{ group | map(attribute='id') | join(',') }}{% if not loop.last %} && {% endif %}{% endfor %}{{ '\n' }}```

2. Run `wait_subagents`. You are then told each subagent's hidden-test results, and its files appear under `subagents/<name>/`.
3. For any problem a subagent did not solve, fix it yourself in `problems/<id>/solution.py`.

Working this way finishes the job much sooner, stops one hard problem from using up the turns the others need, and leaves your own turns for checking and fixing. It is advice, not a rule: how you split the problems, and whether you keep some for yourself, is your decision. A solution a subagent got right counts for you automatically; you do not need to copy it. You are judged on how many problems end up solved and on how quickly the whole job finishes.
